---
# RUNTIME INVARIANTS -- statements about what the pipeline's output IS, checked continuously,
# rather than about how its machinery is running.
#
# Every other application rule watches a mechanism: a queue, a cursor, a scrape, a render. Each
# of the five here states something that must stay true of the RESULT, whatever path produced it
# -- the first, second and fourth after real incidents that left every mechanism panel clean:
#
#   CityServingShortOfCorpus        a city serves far fewer films than its corpus has
#                                   (2026-08-30: 300+ US cities for ~2h after the metro split, and
#                                   the Bay for 38h after its slug rename -- 164 of 418 films)
#   CityDarkInBothTiers             a city with films all week has none, in the corpus or the site
#                                   (Butte, MT dark 2026-09-07..09 with nothing alerting)
#   CinemaContentStaleVenuesGrowing more venues go a week without a film every day
#   ReadModelContentMismatch        a stored card differs from a fresh projection of its row
#                                   (Troy / 2046 / Glastonbury served August showtimes for 10 days)
#   ShareCardPointersUnresolvable   web_movies.shareCard names a file that is not on disk at that
#                                   version
#
# THE `country` LABEL comes from the scrape (scrape-kinowo-apps.yaml), not the application, as in
# every sibling file: the worker's own `country` becomes `exported_country`.
groups:
  - name: runtime-invariants
    rules:
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # SERVED EQUALS CORPUS, PER CITY.
      #
      # `ReadModelServingShortOfCorpus` compares the COUNTRY sums and `ReadModelServingDiffers
      # FromCorpus` the per-city differences summed per country -- both good at "the country is
      # off" and both blind to "one city is badly off": the Bay's 254 missing films
      # (2026-08-30T21:34Z) were under 1% of the US's per-city total, far under the first rule's
      # 3%, and the second (added a week later) excuses a difference that began in a restart for
      # up to two hours. A city-level hole is a whole metro's page showing a fraction of its
      # films, which is the user-visible failure.
      #
      # NO NEW SERIES. Both halves already exist per city -- kinowo_worker_movies_served (the
      # corpus census) and kinowo_web_movies_served (the web's in-memory read model), ~940 cities
      # across five countries. This records ONLY the cities in violation (bounded by the problem,
      # empty when healthy) and their count per country, which is all the alert needs; a worker-
      # side gauge would have had to re-derive the web's view to say the same thing.
      #
      # 5 FILMS AND 20% TOGETHER. The absolute floor keeps a four-film town that is one film short
      # (25%) quiet; the relative one keeps New York, whose attribution between neighbouring cities
      # legitimately wobbles by a few films, quiet. Replayed at a one-minute step over
      # 2026-09-10..09-24, the longest any city stayed past both was 8 minutes (starogard-gdanski,
      # a boot race), so `for: 30m` never fired; over 2026-08-30..09-01 it fired for 300+ US cities
      # at 17:30 (the metro split, recovered in ~2h) and for san-francisco-bay-area from 21:34 for
      # 38 hours.
      #
      # `last_over_time(...[10m])` on both halves so a worker or web restart (gauges absent ~3
      # minutes) neither drops the pending alert nor reads as a city with nothing served.
      - record: country_city:readmodel_served_short_of_corpus:films
        expr: >-
          (
            max by (country, city) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"}[10m]))
            - max by (country, city) (last_over_time(kinowo_web_movies_served{scope="all"}[10m]))
          ) >= 5
          and on (country, city)
          (
            (
              max by (country, city) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"}[10m]))
              - max by (country, city) (last_over_time(kinowo_web_movies_served{scope="all"}[10m]))
            )
            / max by (country, city) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"}[10m]))
          ) >= 0.2

      - record: country:readmodel_cities_short_of_corpus:count
        expr: count by (country) (country_city:readmodel_served_short_of_corpus:films)

      - alert: CityServingShortOfCorpus
        expr: country:readmodel_cities_short_of_corpus:count > 0
        # THIRTY MINUTES: past the longest benign episode in the replay (8 minutes) almost four
        # times over, and still a fraction of the Bay's 38 hours.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $value }} {{ $labels.country }} cit(y/ies) serve at least 20% (and 5+) fewer films than the corpus has"
          description: >-
            For 30 minutes, in each of these cities the web read model has served at least 5 and
            at least 20% fewer films than the worker's corpus census says the city has
            (kinowo_web_movies_served vs kinowo_worker_movies_served, scope="all"). Name the
            cities with `topk(10, country_city:readmodel_served_short_of_corpus:films{country="{{
            $labels.country }}"})`. Many cities at once after a deploy is an id-scheme or city-model
            change orphaning projected rows (2026-08-30's metro split); ONE city is usually a slug
            rename -- the rows are filed under the old slug, see City.renamedSlugs and
            WebReadModel's former-slug fallback. Re-projection heals it at the scrape cadence (14h
            in the US), so do not wait it out.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # MORE VENUES GO QUIET EVERY DAY.
      #
      # kinowo_worker_cinema_content_stale_venues counts roster venues whose last content-bearing
      # scrape is over a week old (CinemaContentCensus). Its LEVEL means little: closures are not
      # modelled (no flag on Cinema says "shut for the season"), so dormant venues sit in it all
      # winter, and DE's quietest venue alone was 1,383h on 2026-09-24. Its GROWTH is the signal: a
      # client-wide parser break (one skin change, every venue on it) adds venues day after day,
      # while every scrape keeps succeeding and the scrape-staleness rules stay green.
      #
      # TODAY AGAINST YESTERDAY'S LOWEST, held a day. `min_over_time(...[1d] offset 1d)` is the
      # least the count was over the day before, so a step up stays visible for up to 48 hours and
      # the day-long `for:` completes on it; a count that falls back within the day never pages.
      # `min by (country)` across instances because each pod publishes the same archive-derived
      # figure under its own `instance`, and a restart renames it.
      #
      # 3 VENUES AND 5% TOGETHER, and NOT REPLAYED: the gauge is new, and the one it sits beside
      # (the oldest age) is a single venue that cannot reconstruct a count. Chosen conservatively,
      # so one seasonal venue closing never pages and a large roster must grow by a twentieth of
      # its stale count in a day. Revisit once the gauge has a fortnight of history.
      - alert: CinemaContentStaleVenuesGrowing
        expr: >-
          (
            max by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_cinema_content_stale_venues[1h]))
            - min by (country) (min_over_time(kinowo_worker_cinema_content_stale_venues[1d] offset 1d))
          ) >= 3
          and on (country)
          (
            max by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_cinema_content_stale_venues[1h]))
            - min by (country) (min_over_time(kinowo_worker_cinema_content_stale_venues[1d] offset 1d))
          ) >= 0.05 * min by (country) (min_over_time(kinowo_worker_cinema_content_stale_venues[1d] offset 1d))
        for: 24h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} has {{ $value }} more venue(s) a week without a film than at any point the day before"
          description: >-
            kinowo_worker_cinema_content_stale_venues has stayed at least 3 (and 5%) above the
            previous day's lowest reading for a full day. Venues that scrape successfully and find
            nothing look white on /uptime, exactly like a cinema closed for the season, so check
            WHICH venues joined: the cinema_scrapes archive's lastSuccess per venue, grouped by
            scraper client. Several venues of one client going quiet together is a parser break
            (the 2026-09-04 Java `\w` regex took four PL venues, only one of them white); scattered
            venues in a region in autumn is a season closing.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # STORED CONTENT MATCHES A FRESH RE-READ.
      #
      # The worker's hourly content audit (ReadModelContentAudit) samples 50 cards, compares each
      # stored document and its screenings rows FIELD BY FIELD with what the projection derives
      # from the source row now, and re-checks every difference 15 minutes later; only one still
      # there is `confirmed`. That catches a card that exists and is wrong -- what every id-level
      # check and every served-count rule passes.
      #
      # 2% OF WHAT WAS AUDITED over six hours, with at least 100 audited. NOT replayable directly
      # (the audit is new); approximated from the rolling content check, which rewrites every row
      # whose stored projection drifted from its source once a day: its drift writes over
      # 2026-09-10..09-24 peaked at 13 documents a day in the US against a ~2,300-row corpus, a
      # standing wrong-row share of at most ~0.6% -- the rate this audit would sample. 2% is over
      # three times that ceiling. The floor of 100 audited keeps a country whose worker has only
      # just started from paging on its first two confirmations.
      - alert: ReadModelContentMismatch
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_readmodel_content_audit_confirmed_total[6h]))
            / sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_readmodel_content_audited_total[6h]))
          ) > 0.02
          and on (country) sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_readmodel_content_audited_total[6h])) >= 100
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of sampled read-model cards differ from a fresh projection of their row"
          description: >-
            The content audit found stored web_movies / web_screenings documents that still
            differed from ReadModelProjection's output for their source row 15 minutes after first
            sight. The worker log names each card and the fields ("read-model-content audit: <card>
            still differs ..."). A deploy that changed what the projection derives re-projects the
            whole corpus by itself (worker log "read-model derivation pass", ~8 minutes from the
            first sweep 5 minutes after boot); a pass that could not read every row says so and
            runs again next sweep. If none ran and the differing fields are what a recent deploy
            changed, the fixture corpus did not exercise that change, so no new derivation was
            named: append one to ReadModelDerivation.History (full scope) and redeploy.
            A card the rolling content check has not reached yet is repaired within a day; one
            that stays wrong while its row does not change is a write the projector believes
            landed and the store did not keep (the swallowed-write bug fixed in dfaba5ba8) --
            restart the worker to drop its memo, then find the write path.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # SHARE CARDS RESOLVABLE.
      #
      # The worker's hourly share-card audit (ShareCardAudit) samples 50 films whose web_movies
      # document names a card and checks the named file exists, is that film's, and carries the
      # URL's `?v=` version; a miss is re-checked 15 minutes later, past a render's own
      # re-projection. og:image is that URL verbatim and preview caches keep what they fetch for a
      # month.
      #
      # 5% OF WHAT WAS AUDITED over six hours, with at least 100 audited. Not replayable (new
      # audit, and nothing existing measures the pointer). The pipeline's own design says the
      # confirmed count should be zero -- the janitor re-projects any film whose card it retires,
      # and every finished render re-projects its film -- so 5% leaves room for a race nobody has
      # thought of while still firing at once on the shapes that matter: a lost or remounted card
      # directory reads 100%.
      - alert: ShareCardPointersUnresolvable
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_audit_confirmed_total[6h]))
            / sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_audited_total[6h]))
          ) > 0.05
          and on (country) sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_audited_total[6h])) >= 100
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of sampled share-card pointers do not resolve"
          description: >-
            web_movies.shareCard names a card file that is missing from the country's share-card
            directory, belongs to another film, or is at a different version than the URL's ?v=,
            and still did 15 minutes later. The worker log lists each film ("share-card audit:
            <film> still differs ..."). Every film missing its file at once is the directory: is
            /var/lib/kinowo/share-cards/<cc> still mounted on the worker's node, and did the pod
            move to a node without it? A version mismatch is a render whose re-projection never
            ran (ShareCardFollowUp); a missing file on a film still pointing at it is a prune
            that did not re-project it (ShareCardJanitor's refresh).

  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # A CITY THAT WENT DARK IN BOTH TIERS.
  #
  # Every served-count rule compares the web with the corpus, so none can see a city whose CORPUS
  # emptied too: the web serves exactly what the worker has, which is nothing. Butte, MT went from
  # 18 showtimes on 2026-09-05 to 0 at 03:00Z on 09-07 -- its venue's scrape stopped adding the new
  # week while the old one ran out -- and stayed dark until 04:00Z on 09-09, with no alert: the
  # Grafana `kinowo-movies-served-city-empty` rule deliberately stands down when the corpus is empty
  # too (ab9da30ca, after three thin-venue false pages), and this is the case it hands over.
  #
  # "IT HAD FILMS FOR SIX DAYS" IS WHAT TELLS BUTTE FROM A SMALL TOWN'S WEEK. Replayed at a 15-minute
  # step over 2026-08-30..09-24, 46 zero spells followed a week with 72+ hours of films, and most
  # are WEEKLY: Plentywood, MT is dark 48 hours from every Sunday night, Harvey, ND 27.5 hours, until
  # the next week's programme is published. None of those towns has films six days running, so the
  # condition is: films in every sample from eight days ago to two days ago (the recorded series
  # below, gaps ignored -- a worker restart is not a zero), none in either tier now, held 12 hours.
  # That replay fires six times in 25 days: Butte (09-08, a day before it recovered -- the replay
  # has no data from before 08-30 to fire it sooner), Richfield UT 3h, Sassnitz DE 13h, Hood River
  # OR 8.5h, and Mount Ayr IA with Southeast Alaska for 36h on 09-21 -- each a city whose programme
  # a week earlier had run all week. Weighed against 12 episodes for "zero now but films at this
  # hour last week" and 16 for five days instead of six.
  #
  # IT RESOLVES TWO DAYS AFTER THE CITY WENT DARK, dark or not: the six-day window then reaches into
  # the darkness itself. Forty-eight hours of a warning, re-sent every 12 hours, is not missed.
  #
  # ITS OWN GROUP, AT FIVE MINUTES, because of the six-day window: ~1,000 cities x 1,728 recorded
  # samples per evaluation, where the raw gauge at the default interval would be ten times that. The
  # recorded series needs EIGHT DAYS of history before the alert can fire at all.
  #
  # A CITY THAT LEFT THE ROSTER is not here: the worker stops counting it and the series goes, rather
  # than reading 0 (see `kinowo-movies-served-swing`'s "A PAGE THAT LEFT THE ROSTER").
  - name: runtime-invariants-dark-cities
    interval: 5m
    rules:
      - record: country_city:worker_movies_served:films
        expr: max by (country, city) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"}[10m]))

      - alert: CityDarkInBothTiers
        expr: >-
          country_city:worker_movies_served:films == 0
          unless on (country, city)
            (max by (country, city) (last_over_time(kinowo_web_movies_served{scope="all"}[10m])) > 0)
          and on (country, city)
            (min_over_time(country_city:worker_movies_served:films[6d] offset 2d) > 0)
          and on (country, city)
            (last_over_time(country_city:worker_movies_served:films[1h] offset 8d) > 0)
        for: 12h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.city }} ({{ $labels.country }}) has served no film for 12 hours, after six days that all had films"
          description: >-
            Neither the worker's corpus (kinowo_worker_movies_served) nor the web
            (kinowo_web_movies_served) has a film for {{ $labels.city }}, although it had films in
            every sample from eight days ago to two days ago -- so this is not a small town's weekly
            gap between programmes. The web is serving what the corpus has; the corpus lost the
            city. Check its venues on /uptime (a scrape that keeps succeeding empty looks white)
            and `kinowo_worker_showtimes{city="{{ $labels.city }}"}` over the last three days: a
            steady run-down to zero with no new week landing is a venue whose scrape stopped
            finding its next programme -- a moved listing page, a changed upstream, a venue that
            closed. It resolves on its own two days after the city went dark; that is not a fix.
